## Lifecycle and Ownership

Creating, altering, and dropping policies requires a superuser or the `sys:secadmin` role. Policies have no SQL owner,
so the resource has no `owner` attribute.

Deletion runs `DROP RLS POLICY` without `CASCADE`, so Redshift refuses to drop a policy that is still attached.
Referencing the policy from its attachments makes Terraform detach them first when everything is destroyed. A policy
that uses lookup tables also needs `GRANT SELECT ... TO RLS POLICY` on them.

## Reconciliation

Refresh reads the policy from `svv_rls_policy`; an update runs `ALTER RLS POLICY ... USING` for a new predicate, which
keeps the policy's attachments, and re-reads the catalog to verify the result; every other change replaces the policy; a
change to `column` or `alias` replaces it under the same name, because `ALTER RLS POLICY` cannot change the `WITH`
clause.

| Change                                                                                                                                                          | Result                                                            |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `database` or `name`                                                                                                                                            | replaces the policy: the provider never moves or renames a policy |
| `column` block added, removed, or reordered                                                                                                                     | replaces the policy                                               |
| `column.name` changed only in case, or `column.type` respelled with the same canonical type, such as `varchar(64)` or `character varying(64)` for `VARCHAR(64)` | no change; the plan keeps the stored spelling                     |
| `column.name` or `column.type`, any other change                                                                                                                | replaces the policy                                               |
| `alias` changed only in case                                                                                                                                    | no change; the plan keeps the stored spelling                     |
| `alias` set to a value other than the stored alias, ignoring case                                                                                               | replaces the policy                                               |
| `alias` removed                                                                                                                                                 | no SQL; the current alias stays and is still reported             |
| `column` or `alias` known only during apply                                                                                                                     | replaces the policy                                               |
| `predicate`                                                                                                                                                     | `ALTER RLS POLICY ... USING`                                      |

A replacement keeps the policy name, so the attachments' references to it do not change, and `DROP RLS POLICY` refuses
the policy while they stay attached. Give every attachment `replace_triggered_by` on the policy's `column` and `alias`,
as in the `redshift_rls_policy_attachment` example: Terraform then detaches the attachments, replaces the policy, and
attaches them again. Referencing the whole policy instead would also replace the attachments on every predicate change.

**Drift.** Refresh compares the catalog columns, alias, and predicate with the state. Redshift stores the predicate in a
rewritten form (`svv_rls_policy.polqual`), so state keeps the configured text while the catalog text still matches
`definition_fingerprint`, a hash of it with whitespace collapsed that is recorded at apply time. A predicate changed
outside Terraform appears as the catalog text, and the next plan restores the configured one with `ALTER RLS POLICY`.
Columns or an alias that differ from the catalog beyond spelling, as after the policy was re-created outside
Terraform, are stored as the catalog reports them, and the next plan replaces the policy; an omitted `alias` follows
the catalog. A dropped policy is removed from state, and the next plan creates it again.

